  "opening": "Chapter 7 turned reversible function evaluation into phase oracles. We now ask how to construct such operations from gates acting on one or two qubits.",
  "opening_continuation": "Circuit identities lead to phase markings and reflections. Controlled-gate constructions then establish universality, while parameter counting, approximation, and physical constraints show what implementation costs.",
  "intro_continuation": "Classical reversible circuits are complete once we have the Toffoli gate. Can a fixed set of one- and two-qubit gates similarly build every unitary on \\(n\\) qubits, and how many gates does that take?"
}
